Fetch a URL as compact Markdown (default), text, or HTML (max {{ .MaxFetchSizeKB }}KB output; 5MB input); no AI processing. HTML-to-Markdown conversion removes scripts, styles, and hidden markup while preserving headings, lists, tables, code, and links. Prefer Markdown for readable pages to reduce tokens; choose HTML explicitly when markup is needed. For analysis or extraction use agentic_fetch.
{{- if .GhAvailable }} For GitHub content when an exact repo, issue, or PR link is provided, use `gh` CLI in bash instead.{{- end }}

All requests use recent Google Chrome navigation headers without a Crux or CLI user agent. Normal mode is anonymous and remains the default. Request `mode: user` only when the user asks or browser authentication is needed. User mode always requires explicit user consent for this request or the current session, even with automatic tool approval enabled. The user selects a browser profile on the execution host, and fetch copies matching cookies into memory. Redirects use matching cookies from the same selected profile. If Cloudflare requires a browser check, Crux opens the challenged URL in the user's browser, waits for the user to finish or cancel, recopies cookies from the approved profile, and retries once. Cookies are never written back to the browser, and cookie values are redacted from returned text and diagnostics. This is HTTP fetching with a user-completed browser challenge relay, not general browser automation or JavaScript execution. Denied, cancelled, or unavailable user mode does not silently fall back to anonymous access.
